## Working rules

{% if include_backlog %}- Agent work is defined before it starts: a backlog task (`backlog-py task
  create`, ids `{{ task_prefix }}-…`) stating what done means — acceptance
  criteria or a definition of done. One task per unit of work.
{% else %}- Agent work is defined before it starts: write down what done means —
  acceptance criteria or a definition of done. One task per unit of work.
{% endif %}
- Agent work happens isolated: one worktree per task (`wt switch <branch
  named from the task id>`); never edit a shared checkout for task work.
  Start/finish procedure: `docs/task-workflow.md`.
- The fast loop never needs asking: every commit is queued for background
  review (roborev). Before claiming work done, check open findings for the
  branch (skill: `review-findings`) and fix what is real.
- The slow loop runs on demand (`mise run review-deep`) and when scheduled by
  the maintainer. Its report lands in `docs/reviews/`; its retro section may
  assign tasks — treat those as normal tasks.
- Repetitive AI usage is a candidate for deterministic tooling. When you
  notice a pattern repeating across sessions, record it in
  `docs/experiments.md` instead of doing it by hand a fourth time.
- Every skill you reference must be declared in `agents.toml` (dotagents).
  If an instruction names a skill that is not installed, that is a defect —
  report it, do not improvise the skill.
- Do not add secrets or machine-specific paths to committed configs.
  Personal tool configuration belongs to the user level, not this project.
- `.alint.yml` enforces repository shape; its `agent` output carries fix
  instructions — run what it says. `AGENTS.md` here is kept in step with the
  enforced rules; do not edit the managed section by hand.
- A `--help` that does anything except print help is a broken command: stop
  using it and never probe its surface that way again. It is only a
  convention, and some CLIs ignore it once a subcommand is present — the flag
  falls through to the normal parser and the command runs. The failure is
  silent because the command succeeds and reports success, so nothing
  distinguishes the probe from an intentional run.
  Ask the tool for help at the level that documents its subcommands (e.g.
  `tool help`, or the parent command with no arguments), then read the source
  or the man page. Never append `--help` to a subcommand whose behaviour you
  have not verified.
  Confirm the interface before relying on it, and record a tool's measured
  behaviour as version-pinned — the convention may hold in one release and
  not the next.
{% if include_lore %}- Long-term knowledge lives in `.lore.md`, curated by lore; treat it as
  read-only context. It is a *render* of a database, not the store: hand-editing
  or hand-merging it loses work, because the next `lore data export` discards
  anything the database never held. Change knowledge through the lore CLI only.
  Worktrees can fork the store into a second project row, so the render may show
  a partial view; reconcile with `lore data merge` then `lore data export`, and
  check for the duplicate row before trusting what `.lore.md` shows.
  `lore help` documents the CLI — never `lore <subcommand> --help`.{% endif %}

## Commands

- `mise run install` — bootstrap tools and managed state (dotagents install)
- `mise run review-deep` — slow loop
- `mise run roborev-status` / `roborev-fix` — fast loop findings
- `mise run check` — alint + project checks

